# How many tests each suite must not fall below. Read by `scripts/check`.
#
# **Why a number in a file** (`docs/BACKLOG.md` B63): on 2026-09-12 one commit deleted
# `crates/ipc/tests/profile.rs` — 647 lines, 27 tests — in the same diff that added
# `progress.rs`, and every gate stayed green for four days. `cargo test` reports what ran and
# never what stopped existing: a deleted test is not a dimmed suite, it is a shorter one, and it
# makes no sound at all. The property this pins is the one that incident broke: **a commit that
# removes tests must not be able to come out greener than one that does not.**
#
# **It counts tests that exist, not tests that passed** — passed plus failed plus ignored on the
# Rust side, the parenthesised total on the Vitest one. A test that starts being skipped by the
# harness is a test that stopped existing for this purpose, and should be as loud as a deleted
# one. A `test-support` skip is a different thing and is counted in its own summary: those tests
# ran and said what they could not reach.
#
# **Machine-independent by construction**, which is what makes a committed number honest: every
# real-data test skips rather than vanishing when its sample is missing, so the totals are the
# same on a machine with the game and on one without. Checked across the two: 992 on both before
# the 27 came back.
#
# **Raising it is a deliberate line in a commit.** The check never fails for a suite that grew —
# that would fail every commit adding a test — it prints the new number and the line to paste.
# The limit of that, stated rather than discovered: a floor nobody raises goes stale, so adding
# two hundred tests and later deleting fifty stays quiet. Closing that needs a per-commit
# comparison, which is CI, and there is none here by decision.
#
#
# **Lowered on 2026-09-20**, and this is the deliberate line the paragraph above asks for.
# Rust −15: the fifteen tests of `crates/design-export` went with the crate. UI −4: four tests
# whose subject stopped existing — the Collection fixture's "pack or synthetic" source, which
# has only one answer now; the wiki index asked for art a second time; and the two that
# guarded `packIconUrl` against the game's sprites coming back. That last guard is not lost,
# it moved and got wider: `scripts/check-no-game-assets.mjs` fails on any tracked picture, not
# just on one the design pack would have carried.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-20**, Rust +2: the window on T. Eden beating Mother closed half of B20's
# block, and the two new tests are what it bought — where those cells are, and that Mother's
# group of 34 tiles end to end without a hole. Both are sample-free arithmetic, so they count
# the same on a machine without the game.
#
# **Lowered on 2026-09-20**, UI −5. Five tests whose subject stopped existing when the Floor
# screen went from three answers at once to one at a time: `toggled` and its four (there is
# nothing to toggle in a choice of one), and one of the colour map's (a target has one colour
# now instead of three steps with an ink each, because the rank is how full the square is and
# there is no digit left to make readable).
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-20**, UI +8: the eight tests of `shift`, the arrows that move the whole
# drawing one cell under the grid. Seven of them are about the one thing that function has to
# get right — refusing rather than dropping a room over the edge — because there is no undo on
# that screen.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-20**, Rust +8: the eight tests of B68, which gave the Ultra Secret target
# something to answer. Seven are in `crates/floor/tests/solve.rs` and one in `rules.rs`; all
# eight were written after the code, so all eight were shown red by mutating the three new
# constraints in turn — the dedup in `reached_rooms`, `noPaintedNeighbour` and
# `redRoomForbiddenNeighbour` — rather than by being written first.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-20**, UI +6: 3.13a's, the responsive frame. Two on the window floor — the
# constant, and the Tauri config agreeing with it, which is the one thing standing between two
# files that declare the same number and cannot see each other — and three on the page thresholds:
# that they are declared in the CSS at the px the constants say, that they ascend, and that the
# window floor falls inside compact. The sixth is not new, it is the old
# `--spacing-virtual-rows-body` test **rewritten rather than deleted**: its subject — a token
# capping every virtualized list at 35rem — stopped existing when the scroll body started taking
# the height that is left, so what it pins now is the replacement, `min-h-0 flex-1` on the box and
# no `max-h-` left in it. Rust does not move: nothing in that sub-project is Rust except two keys
# in `tauri.conf.json`, and it is a UI test that reads them.
#
# **The six entries above are one day's worth, and they are written as deltas rather than as
# `1150 → 1158`.** They landed on three branches at once and were merged here: an absolute number
# on each side is a number measured against a different base, and the pair of them conflicted in
# this file for exactly that reason — twice, the second time with 3.13a, which had written its own
# entry as an arrow and had to rewrite it as a delta to resolve. A delta survives a merge; an arrow
# between two totals does not. The totals below are the ones `scripts/check` counted on the merge
# itself.
# **Raised on 2026-09-21**, Rust +12: the key a boss portrait answers to. Ten unit tests beside
# `merge_keys` — one per tier, one per way two rows can want the same key — and two on the
# installed game: that a contested key draws the boss whose page it is, and that the twenty
# pages no file name reaches are reached by their title. The contested set is read from the
# catalog rather than listed, so a patch that contests a fifth key is covered the day it ships.
# UI does not move: nothing in this one is UI.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-21**, Rust +4 and UI +2, all of B21. Rust: two on the new shape of a cell
# (the reading it carries, and that reading's JSON), two on real data — the matched window of
# 09-12, and the property over the series that says the online bit belongs to the run and not
# to the day. The two real-data ones passed the moment they were written, which is what a test
# of a measurement does, so both were mutated until they failed and the failures were read
# before they were trusted. UI +2 and not more: eight tests of the bit rules stayed at the same
# count but changed subject — they used to decode a mask and now check that the reading arrives
# intact, because the decoding moved to Rust — and the two added are the probe that says no
# decoder is left there. **Written as a delta and resolved as one**: it met 3.13a's six UI tests
# in this very file, which is the third time the paragraph above has been paid for.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-21**, UI +7: card #57, the Wiki's three views. Five are `wikiOutline`'s —
# the index beside a page's text, and the rule that a page with one section gets none, which is
# the only judgment in the whole sub-project. Two are `rows`'s: the new `--spacing-row-wiki`
# token and the number the virtualizer positions its rows with, held together the way the other
# two row heights already are. Rust does not move: nothing in the Wiki's three views is Rust.
# **A delta again, and it met B21's in this file** — the fourth time. Neither side could have
# written a total: B21 raised UI from 747 to 749 while this branch was raising it to 754 from
# the same 747, and 749 + 7 is the only number either of them agrees with.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-21**, Rust +27: card #58, the completion widget as the Completion
# screen's emblem. Three in `catalog` — a frame now carries *where* its layer draws it, which
# is the one number a picture made of layers needs and a single icon never did. Eight on
# `overlay`, the composition itself, and six of those eight are about the ways it must not
# lose a piece: a transparent pixel, a half-transparent one, an offset that hangs over an
# edge, one entirely off it, and a piece that is not a PNG at all. Six on `widget_source`,
# three on the address, four on the matrix that asks for it, and three on the installed game
# — where the last of those exists because the other two cannot fail on their own: it
# composes the bare paper and the full one and asserts they differ, so "96×96, fine" is never
# read off an instrument nobody has shown able to speak. UI does not move in this commit; its
# half comes with the screen.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-21**, UI +3: card #58's other half, and all three are the fourth KPI —
# complete *bosses*, the column's reading of what "complete characters" says down a row. One
# pins it on the reference profile, where the answer is **zero**: counted on the fixture's
# digit strings with a script of its own, the fullest column is Mom's Heart at 21 of 34. A
# zero passes for the wrong reasons too, so the second builds a column that *is* complete and
# the third builds one nothing can be read from — the `0/0` an equality alone would call
# finished. Nothing else in the band or the grid is logic: the emblem's rule is Rust's, the
# bars and the pinned columns are CSS, and neither has a test here that would only be
# restating the template.
#
# **Raised on 2026-09-22**, Rust +6: `centre_opaque`, and it exists because the emblem was
# looked at in the real window and was visibly off-centre in its frame. The game's crop is
# not centred on what it draws — the clean paper covers x 0-84, y 3-82 of its 96x96 and the
# bloodied one x 0-87, y 1-82, so the two tiers are off by different amounts and no written
# offset fits both. Four of the six are the ways a re-centring goes wrong rather than the
# happy path: a drawing already centred that must not drift, an odd margin that must leave
# the extra pixel on one side instead of splitting the drawing, a canvas that must not change
# size, and a count of opaque pixels that must come out the same — the failure being a shift
# that pushes part of the picture off the edge, which looks like a crop nobody asked for and
# on this picture means losing a column.

# **Raised on 2026-09-22**, Rust +18 and UI +6, B70 and B69 together — the two ways a row can
# draw the wrong picture. B70 is Rust only, 15 of the 18: seven on the versus screen, where
# the crop of a boss portrait is read from the game's own scene rather than guessed, two on a
# row carrying it, three on the installed game and three on the reference itself. One of the
# three real-data ones found something on its first run — Dogma's scene asks for 208 pixels of
# a 192-pixel file — which is why it checks that a crop starts inside its file rather than
# that it ends inside one. B69 is the other three Rust and all six UI: the stand-in picture,
# its round trip, the file it names checked against the archives, the state that carries its
# URL and the one that does not, and six on the order the three layers are drawn in — which is
# the whole of the bug, since "nothing" and "we could not find it" used to draw the same
# square.


# **Raised on 2026-09-22**, UI +1: the location the app opens on. It moved from Goals to
# Completion, and until today nothing said what it was — `defaultLocation` is read by the `/`
# redirect and by a first launch with no saved tabs, and either could have been changed
# without a word. Rust does not move: which screen opens is a frontend constant and no
# command knows about it.

# **Raised on 2026-09-22**, UI +9 net, and the net is the point: 23 arrived and 14 left, in the
# cycle that made Obiettivi and the Plan one screen. Arrived: 11 on `rowModel`, the one module
# that decides what a row says; 4 on `queueExtras`, what the queue around a row adds; 5 on
# `addPaneState`, which of the left pane's four states is showing; 3 on the retired-name map,
# where a stored tab on the Plan is carried to Obiettivi instead of being dropped in silence.
# Left: the 8 of `goalCard.test.ts`, the 5 of `planNow.test.ts`, and 1 of `queueRows`'s.
#
# **Only 6 of those 14 are a loss of coverage, and none of the 14 is a loss of a guard.** The 8
# are `goalCard`'s and they were **carried into `rowModel.test.ts`, not deleted** — B28's Tainted
# name, the 231 nameless nodes, the slot that must go nowhere: every one is asserted in the new
# file, against the function that replaced the old one. The other 6 go because their subject
# does. `planNow` fed the "in your Plan" reminder, which a page that could not see the queue
# needed and a page with the queue beside it does not; `queueSummary` fed the band of three
# counts the owner declined. A test whose subject stopped existing is not coverage dropped, it
# is coverage with nothing left to be about — and saying which of the two a deletion was is the
# whole reason this file is prose and not a number.
#
# Rust does not move: the cycle is frontend end to end, and the spec's §7 says that was checked
# rather than hoped — everything the new row draws was already on the wire.

RUST_TESTS=1227
UI_TESTS=775
